      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Vicksburg, Shreveport and Texas Railroad reached a terminus at Tallulah Station in 1857, spurring cotton trade and settlement growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mound_louisiana",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The mound, like many in the Delta, was built by indigenous peoples for ceremonial or residential purposes, possibly linked to Poverty Point culture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mound_louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Great Mississippi Flood of 1927 inundated Madison Parish, severely affecting Mound and surrounding communities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mound_louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mound’s early 20th‑century economy hinged on agriculture, timber harvesting, and a nearby sawmill that supported a general store and hotel.",
